  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThe main event is within reach. The much advertised and anticipated \ncold front is forecast to move through South Texas Friday. The \nnortherly wind shift is anticipated during the overnight hours but \nthe quick increase in surface wind speeds will occur during the \nmorning commute and into mid-morning. These winds then quickly \nincrease to gale conditions over the bays and open Gulf waters, \nprompting the upgrade to a Gale Warning. Inland, wind gusts \nthroughout Friday afternoon/evening have a 30-50% chance of \nexceeding 40 mph (and lingering longer for those along the Coastal \nBend/Victoria Crossroads). As such, a Wind Advisory has been issued \nto begin Friday morning, and ending across the Brush Country at 7 PM \nFriday, across the Coastal Plains at 1 AM Saturday, and on the \nbarrier islands 1 PM Saturday. The winds in combination with RH \nvalues dropping to near or below 30% across the Rio Grande Plains \nFriday afternoon, but this will be limited to the late afternoon. As \nof now, no fire headline is anticipated, but one will be \nreconsidered this evening.\n\nWith a cold front, one usually wonders if there will be \nprecipitation tagging along. However, with this specific set up, \nmuch of the upper-level energy will remain north and east of the Red \nRiver. Additionally, a weak coastal trough is surprisingly advecting \nrelatively-drier air towards the coast through Friday afternoon. \nWhile surface convergence ahead of the front would spark more \nwidespread showers, it's looking like the spatial coverage of any \nshowers will be more isolated and generally will be limited to \nbefore noon. For that reason, Friday night football games will see \nmost bleachers drying out by game time and quite the struggle for \nkickers given any possible crosswind factors!\n\nWith respect to temperatures, this will not be a sudden drop in \ntemperatures with the increase in wind speed. It'll take some time \nto drop the dewpoint temperatures in the afternoon, but by the time \nfolks start heading out to Friday night outdoor activities, the drop \nin temperature will be more noticeable (especially given the breezy \nconditions). That said, the highs will likely occur early Friday \nmorning, and generally stay in the mid- to upper-70s Friday \nafternoon. Saturday morning, low temperatures will bottom out in the \n50s (60s along the immediate coastline). The last time we had lows \nbelow 60F was back on May 3rd! Temperatures climb to the low to mid-\n70s Saturday afternoon as winds decrease.\n\nIf that wasn't enough, we're also monitoring coastal flooding \nconcerns. While latest guidance puts high tide cycles between 1.3-\n1.5 ft above MSL, even the wave energy won't be persistent enough to \npush wave periods above 8-9 seconds and thus not quite the runup \nGulf beaches saw last week.\n\nAll this to say, our first front will be a welcomed change to some, \nbut treacherous for our mariners. Looking beyond Saturday, below-\nnormal temperatures stick around Sunday with a more easterly wind \ncomponent by mid-week and temperatures climbing towards the 90s \nthanks to building upper-level ridge influence.",
